CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 2 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
A read through a symbolic-size malloc'd pointer goes through CBMC's own
pointer-identity resolution (the #305 fix's value-set dereference), which
wraps a plain indexed access -- not a `with` -- in the same if-chain shape
that `with_index_under_if` was already handling for writes: `ptr == &obj1 ?
obj1[k] : (ptr == &obj2 ? obj2[k] : ...)`. Reading `arr[k]` as a
function-call argument (`pthread_join(tids[0], ...)` here) takes exactly
this path, even when `k` is a compile-time constant -- it is the *pointer*,
not the index, that is symbolic. Recognising only `with` left this read at
the sentinel offset, so a write (which does produce a `with`) and this read
could never be seen as the same location, and the race between
`pthread_create`'s write to `tids[0]` and this read via `pthread_join` was
missed.

`with_index_under_if` now also recognises a bare `ID_index` nested under the
if-chain, extracting its index the same way the `with` case already did.

This is the second of two defects behind the corpus's
`per-thread-array-join-counter-race{,-2,-3,-4}` wrong/unknown answers (the
first, `pthread_create`'s own write being invisible to race detection, is
commit 6ca9e177). Fixing this one does not by itself flip those four corpus
benchmarks either: a third, distinct gap remains for `flags`/`datas`-style
pointer arrays accessed via pointer arithmetic (`ptr + i`) rather than an
`ID_index` expression at all -- see project memory for that part, still open.

See `symbolic_malloc_pointer_identity_index_read_ordered`, the negative
control: same structure, but the write happens-before the read is even
possible (no thread exists yet to race it), and must stay SUCCESSFUL before
and after this fix.
